    #          worksheet has two sheetPr, autoFilter, printOptions, ...", from the
    #          settings pass;
    #       A2C one <autoFilter>, the control for A2;
    #       F3 a row 1048576 with a cell, so M + 1 is past the grid: part 3's
    #          index message, from the print area call;
    #       F3C a row 2 with a cell, the control for F3.
    # Each refusal: exit 2, the error line exactly the message the committed
    # source holds, no stdout, no output file. Then the part's messages, read from
    # the source by constant name, compared as SETS with those reached here and
    # those the log states unreachable, each unreachable one with its reason and
    # the tests that cover it; the two shared messages reached here apart; and
    # usage shapes.
    #
    # CARRIED TO M3 part 3 P3 from M3 part 3 P2's negative script (printed in
    # records/logs/m3p3-p2/negative.log, byte-identical to its copy kept on the
    # disk as <home>/.cache/emend-build/m3p3-p3/gen/negative-template-m3p3-p2.sh;
    # its change lists, P2's IA to ID and the earlier ones, follow below). Every
    # check, control and helper of the frame is that text's. What differs, and
    # why:
    #  JA. The phase, the log directory, the subjects and the build directories
    #      are M3 part 3 P3's. The base is M3 part 3 P2's record commit ("M3 part
    #      3 P2 record:"); the doc commit and the three code commits (the carry,
    #      the crate, the tool) by their full P3 subjects. The spec pin is
    #      specs/0025's blob at P3's doc commit, 338f0e8b. The working file is
    #      printed, and the status pattern allows it modified: planning holds
    #      "Planning rulings during P3" uncommitted. The pattern allows this
    #      phase's logs and its records/m3p3-p3- and records/m3p3-open- stems.
    #  JB. One family, Q0 to F3C, the generator gen-p3.py written for this phase:
    #      the template's sheetData given rows, root children after it, a
    #      relationships prefix on the root, a worksheet relationships part and a
    #      table part, as each case needs. The part's write message reached by
    #      two autoFilter; the overlap and index messages, shared with earlier
    #      parts, reached apart, each with its control.
    #  JC. The part's messages are the constants package.rs defines under the
    #      Cargo feature sheetprops and under sheetprops and metadata together
    #      (gated.py now takes the cfg's text), compared as a SET with the
    #      constants seven bodies return: P2's six and Parts::set_print_titles;
    #      set_print_area and set_auto_filter return the shared index message
    #      alone and are not among them. "a sheet setting is out of range", "a
    #      header or footer text is not valid" and "a print area or title is not
    #      valid" are stated unreachable through the tool (the sample makes no
    #      settings or header call, and its title pair is its constant), each
    #      with the crate tests that cover it and each test's body asserted to
    #      hold the message. TABLE_OVERLAP and INDEX_OUT_OF_RANGE are read by
    #      name apart, each once and not empty; the overlap message is looked for
    #      in the working spec, where "Planning rulings during P3" ruling 1 quotes
    #      it, the committed spec misquoting it (printed, not asserted).
    #  JD. Nothing of P2's carried rules is dropped. This script removes no
    #      directory: the worktree goes by git worktree remove, the scratch
    #      directory is kept on the disk under <build>, and no rm of any form is
    #      run (specs/0024 P2 ruling 1 needs none here).
    #
    # P2's text:
    # Milestone 3 part 3 P2 negative.log (specs/0025-sheet-and-print-settings.md,
    # "P2": "The negative log through the binary: the sample on a fixture with two
    # <pageSetup> (refused, with a control)"). One family, --pagesetup-sample, the
    # gate's host debug binary copied into a detached worktree of the phase commit
    # and checked against gate.log's hash, on fictional packages this log builds
    # from the phase commit's template files. The template's worksheet holds an
    # empty sheetData alone (none of the four elements the sample sets, no
    # sheetPr), its root binds no relationships prefix:
    #
    # (P) --pagesetup-sample:
    #       P0 the bare template: <sheetPr>, <printOptions>, <pageMargins>,
    #          <pageSetup> and <headerFooter> all created, exit 0, its line;
    #       P1 two <pageSetup/> after the sheetData: the part's message, "a
    #          worksheet has two sheetPr, autoFilter, printOptions, ...", from the
    #          settings pass;
    #       P1C one <pageSetup/>, the control for P1;
    #       P2 a <pageSetup/> and then a <pageMargins/>, the pageMargins out of
    #          schema order: the same message;
    #       P2C the <pageMargins/> before the <pageSetup/>, the control for P2;
    #       P3 two <headerFooter/>: the same message;
    #       P3C one <headerFooter/>, the control for P3.
    # Each refusal: exit 2, the error line exactly the message the committed
    # source holds, no stdout, no output file. Then the part's messages, read from
    # the source by constant name, compared as SETS with those reached here and
    # those the log states unreachable, each unreachable one with its reason and
    # the tests that cover it; and usage shapes.
    #
    # CARRIED TO M3 part 3 P2 from M3 part 3 P1's negative script (printed in
    # records/logs/m3p3-p1/negative.log, byte-identical to its copy kept on the
    # disk as <home>/.cache/emend-build/m3p3-p2/gen/negative-template-m3p3-p1.sh;
    # its change lists, P1's HA to HD and the earlier ones, follow below). Every
    # check, control and helper of the frame is that text's. What differs, and
    # why:
    #  IA. The phase, the log directory, the subjects and the build directories
    #      are M3 part 3 P2's. The base is M3 part 3 P1's record commit ("M3 part
    #      3 P1 record:"); the doc commit and the three code commits (the carry,
    #      the crate, the tool) by their full P2 subjects. The spec pin is
    #      specs/0025's blob at P2's doc commit, 6c6e87e3. The working file is
    #      printed, and the status pattern allows it modified: planning holds
    #      "Planning rulings during P2" uncommitted. The pattern allows this
    #      phase's logs and its records/m3p3-p2- and records/m3p3-open- stems.
    #  IB. One family, P0 to P3C, the generator gen-p2.py written for this phase:
    #      the template's sheetData given a tail of root children after it. The
    #      part's write message reached three ways (two pageSetup, one out of
    #      order, two headerFooter), each with its control. P1's apart record of
    #      part 3's two-elements message is dropped: no case here reaches it.
    #  IC. The part's messages are the constants package.rs defines under the
    #      Cargo feature sheetprops, compared as a SET with the constants the six
    #      bodies return: Parts::set_tab_color, set_sheet_view,
    #      set_page_margins, set_page_setup and set_header_footer (the private
    #      fns) and SettingsPass::observe; set_print_options records with no
    #      check and has no private body. "a sheet setting is out of range" and
    #      "a header or footer text is not valid" are stated unreachable through
    #      the tool (the sample's settings and texts are its constants), each
    #      with the crate tests that cover it and each test's body asserted to
    #      hold the message.
    #  ID. Nothing of P1's carried rules is dropped. This script removes no
    #      directory: the worktree goes by git worktree remove, the scratch
    #      directory is kept on the disk under <build>, and no rm of any form is
    #      run (specs/0024 P2 ruling 1 needs none here).
    #
    # P1's text:
    # Milestone 3 part 3 P1 negative.log (specs/0025-sheet-and-print-settings.md,
    # "P1": "The negative log through the binary: the sample on a fixture with two
    # <sheetPr> (refused, with a control)"). One family, --sheetview-sample, the
    # gate's host debug binary copied into a detached worktree of the phase commit
    # and checked against gate.log's hash, on fictional packages this log builds
    # from the phase commit's template files. The template's worksheet holds an
    # empty sheetData alone (no sheetPr, no dimension, no sheetViews), its root
    # binds no relationships prefix:
    #
    # (S) --sheetview-sample:
    #       S0 the bare template: <sheetPr> and <sheetViews> both created, exit 0,
    #          its line;
    #       S1 two <sheetPr/> before the sheetData: the part's new message, "a
    #          worksheet has two sheetPr, ...", from the settings pass;
    #       S1C one <sheetPr/>, the control for S1;
    #       S2 a <dimension> and then a <sheetPr/>, the sheetPr out of schema
    #          order: the same message;
    #       S2C the <sheetPr/> before the <dimension>, the control for S2;
    #       S3 two <sheetViews>, each holding one <sheetView>: part 3's message
    #          for two cols, sheetViews or mergeCells elements;
    #       S3C one <sheetViews>, the control for S3.
    # Each refusal: exit 2, the error line exactly the message the committed
    # source holds, no stdout, no output file. Then the part's messages, read from
    # the source by constant name, compared as SETS with those reached here and
    # those the log states unreachable, the unreachable one with its reason and
    # the test that covers it; and usage shapes.
    #
    # CARRIED TO M3 part 3 P1 from M3 part 2 P3's negative script (printed in
    # records/logs/m3p2-p3/negative.log, byte-identical to its copy kept on the
    # disk under <home>/.cache/emend-build/m3p2-p3/scripts, which this phase keeps
    # as <home>/.cache/emend-build/m3p3-p1/gen/negative-template-m3p2-p3.sh; its
    # change lists GA to GD stand in that log). Every check, control and helper
    # of the frame is that text's (the entry and exit checks, the shape greps,
    # the role checks, the roots, the gate binary's copy and hash, the message
    # reader messages.py and the body reader returns.py with their controls, the
    # spec lookup, run_bin and its predicates, the set comparison, the usage
    # shapes, the byte count, the status and stderr checks). What differs, and
    # why:
    #  HA. The phase, the log directory, the subjects and the build directories
    #      are M3 part 3 P1's. The base is M3 part 2's close-out record commit
    #      ("M3 part 2 close-out record:"); the doc commit and the three code
    #      commits (the carry, the crate, the tool) by their full P1 subjects.
    #      The spec pin is specs/0025's blob at P1's doc commit, c15d68d2. The
    #      working file is printed, and the status pattern allows it modified:
    #      planning holds "Planning rulings during P1" uncommitted. The pattern
    #      allows this phase's logs and its records/m3p3-p1- and records/m3p3-open-
    #      stems.
    #  HB. One family, S0 to S3C, the generator gen-p1.py written for this phase:
    #      the template's sheetData given a head of root children before it. The
    #      new write message reached two ways (two sheetPr, one out of order),
    #      part 3's message once (two sheetViews), each with its control.
    #  HC. The part's messages are the constants package.rs defines under the
    #      Cargo feature sheetprops (a reader printed below takes each const
    #      whose line follows #[cfg(feature = "sheetprops")]), compared as a SET
    #      with the constants the three bodies return: Parts::set_tab_color,
    #      Parts::set_sheet_view (the private fns) and SettingsPass::observe.
    #      Part 3's two-elements message is recorded apart (as P3 recorded the
    #      index message), since it is not the part's. "a sheet setting is out of
    #      range" is stated unreachable through the tool (the sample's colour and
    #      zoom are its constants), with the crate test that covers it and the
    #      test's body asserted to hold the message. "Errors"' other shared
    #      messages are every edit's, not the sample's constants, and earlier
    #      parts' negative logs reach them; they are printed, not accounted.
    #  HD. Nothing of P3's carried rules is dropped. This script removes no
    #      directory: the worktree goes by git worktree remove, the scratch
    #      directory is kept on the disk under <build>, and no rm of any form is
    #      run (specs/0024 P2 ruling 1 needs none here).
    #
    # Usage: m4p1-p1-negative.sh <phase commit> > <file outside the tree>; the
    # caller moves the output to records/logs/m4p1-p1/negative.log.
    set -uo pipefail
    SELF_DIR=$(cd "$(dirname "$0")" && pwd)
    SELF=$SELF_DIR/$(basename "$0")
    # specs/0017 P1 verification ruling 7: this script's own standard error, kept and counted at the end.
    mkdir -p "$SELF_DIR/out" || exit 2
    ERR=$SELF_DIR/out/negative.stderr
    exec 2>"$ERR"
    cd "$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)" || exit 2
    MAIN=$(pwd)
    
    C=${1:?phase commit required}
    unset EMEND_CORPUS_DIR EMEND_KEEP_DIR
    export RUSTUP_AUTO_INSTALL=0 CARGO_TERM_COLOR=never
    PHASE_DIR=$HOME/.cache/emend-build/m4p1-p1
    BUILD_ROOT=${NEGATIVE_BUILD:-$PHASE_DIR/negative}
    SUPERSEDED_DIR=$PHASE_DIR/superseded
    TARGET_DIR=$PHASE_DIR/target
    GATE_LOG=${GATE_LOG:-$PHASE_DIR/logs/gate.log}
    SPEC=specs/0030-carries-and-api-changes.md
    SPEC_WANT=7bc47caecab30ad1de299cea49f967aea018f10c3d0599b8fbd4d77a191332f3
    mkdir -p "$BUILD_ROOT" || exit 2
    # Change YC: made on the disk under <build> and kept after the run.
    B=$(mktemp -d -p "$BUILD_ROOT") || exit 2
    git show "$C:scripts/counting.sh" >"$B/counting.sh" || exit 2
    . "$B/counting.sh"
    LIST=$B/excel-check-list.py
    git show "$C:scripts/excel-check-list.py" >"$LIST" || exit 2
    fail=0
    check() { echo "  $1: $2"; [ "$2" = yes ] || fail=1; }
    yn() { if "$@"; then echo yes; else echo no; fi; }
    # For a condition that must NOT hold: a leading bang inside yn runs a
    # command named by the bang (specs/0001), so a negation has its own helper.
    ynnot() { if "$@"; then echo no; else echo yes; fi; }
    TAB=$(printf '\t')
    
    head_is() { [ "$(git rev-parse HEAD)" = "$1" ]; }
    full_sha() { printf '%s' "$1" | LC_ALL=C "$G" -qE '^[0-9a-f]{40}$'; }
    # The given commit is the phase commit when it is a full sha and HEAD.
    given_ok() { full_sha "$1" && head_is "$1"; }
    if given_ok "$C"; then :; else echo "the phase commit must be a full 40-hex sha that is HEAD, not $C" >&2; exit 2; fi
    NULL_SHA=0000000000000000000000000000000000000000
    case "${C: -1}" in 0) LAST=1 ;; *) LAST=0 ;; esac
    FAKE_SHA=${C:0:39}$LAST
    SHORT_SHA=${C:0:7}
    # This phase's logs and planning's uncommitted spec (change YA).
    OURS="^((\\?\\?| ?[MARCD]) records/logs/m4p1-p1/| M specs/0030-carries-and-api-changes[.]md\$)"
    entry_all=$(run_lines git status --porcelain)
    entry_ours=$(run_matches -E "$OURS" git status --porcelain)
    entry_clean() { [ "$entry_all" != error ] && [ "$entry_ours" != error ] && [ "$entry_all" = "$entry_ours" ]; }
    if entry_clean; then :; else
      echo "the working tree holds changes outside this phase's logs and planning's spec" >&2
      exit 2
    fi
    
    scrub() {
      sed -e "s#$MAIN#<repo>#g" -e "s#$SELF_DIR#<scripts>#g" -e "s#$B#<scratch>/w#g" \
        -e "s#$BUILD_ROOT#<build>#g" -e "s#$SUPERSEDED_DIR#<superseded>#g" \
        -e "s#$TARGET_DIR#<target>#g" -e "s#$PHASE_DIR#<phase>#g" \
        -e "s#$HOME#<home>#g"
    }
    exec 3>&1
    exec > >(scrub >&3)
    SCRUB_PID=$!
    
    echo "command: git rev-parse HEAD"
    echo "invocation: bash <scripts>/$(basename "$0") $*"
    # The environment and path inputs, each on its labelled line.
    echo "input: RERUN_NOTE: ${RERUN_NOTE:-unset}"
    echo "input: RERUN_FILES: ${RERUN_FILES:-unset}"
    echo "input: ANTHROPIC_API_KEY: $([ -n "${ANTHROPIC_API_KEY:-}" ] && echo set || echo unset) (its value never printed)"
    echo "input: HOME: set, read for the paths under it, printed as <home>"
    echo "input: NEGATIVE_BUILD: ${NEGATIVE_BUILD:-unset} (the build root, <build>)"
    echo "input: GATE_LOG: ${GATE_LOG} (the gate log whose DEBUG binaries' hashes this log checks its copies against)"
    echo "about: M4 part 1 P1 phase commit $C; HEAD at run: $(git rev-parse HEAD)"
    echo
    echo "## the three shape greps, over this script, before its log (specs/0001)"
    D='$'
    SHAPE_CHAIN="\\${D}\\((yn|ynnot|is|isnot) .*(&&|\\|\\|)"
    SHAPE_BANG="\\${D}\\((yn|ynnot|is|isnot) !"
    SHAPE_ASSIGN="\\${D}\\((yn|ynnot|is|isnot) [A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*="
    printf '%s\n' "x=${D}(is [ 1 ] && true)" "y=${D}(isnot ! true)" "z=${D}(yn N=1 true)" >"$B/shape-fixture"
    echo "command: count_matches -E <shape> <this script>, and the same over a fixture holding one line of each shape"
    for shape in CHAIN BANG ASSIGN; do
      var=SHAPE_$shape
      n_self=$(count_matches -E "${!var}" "$SELF")
      n_fix=$(count_matches -E "${!var}" "$B/shape-fixture")
      echo "  shape $shape: in this script $n_self; in the fixture $n_fix"
      check "$shape: this script holds none" "$(yn [ "$n_self" = 0 ])"
      check "control: $shape: the same form finds the fixture's one line" "$(yn [ "$n_fix" = 1 ])"
    done
    check "control: ynnot reports no on a passing command" "$(yn [ "$(ynnot true)" = no ])"
    
    echo
    echo "## the phase's commits by role, read from git"
    by_subject() { git log --format='%H' -n 1 -F --grep="$1"; }
    REC=$(by_subject 'M3 part 7 close-out record: the push, the CI run and the scan')
    DOC1=$(by_subject "M4 part 1 P1: commit planning docs (M3 part 7's close-out rulings, the Milestone 4 outline, specs/0030)")
    RULES1=$(by_subject "M4 part 1 P1: specs/0001 sentence on the runner's reused run directory")
    RUNNER1=$(by_subject "M4 part 1 P1: the runner refuses a run directory holding an earlier run")
    LIST1=$(by_subject "M4 part 1 P1: the Excel list leaves out a run directory's error rows")
    echo "command: git log --format=%H -n 1 -F --grep=<the role's full subject>, for each role"
    echo "  M3 part 7's close-out record commit (the base): ${REC:-none}"
    echo "  the doc commit: ${DOC1:-none}"
    echo "  the rules commit (specs/0001's sentence): ${RULES1:-none}"
    echo "  the runner commit (scripts/passthrough-run.sh): ${RUNNER1:-none}"
    echo "  the list commit (scripts/excel-check-list.py, the phase commit): ${LIST1:-none}"
    echo "command: git rev-parse <phase commit>~0 to <phase commit>~4"
    for k in 0 1 2 3 4; do git rev-parse "$C~$k"; done | sed 's/^/    /'
    roles_in_order() {
      local r k=0
      for r in "$LIST1" "$RUNNER1" "$RULES1" "$DOC1" "$REC"; do
        [ -n "$r" ] || return 1
        [ "$(git rev-parse "$C~$k")" = "$r" ] || return 1
        k=$((k + 1))
      done
    }
    check "the list commit, the runner commit, the rules commit, the doc commit and the base, each the next one's parent, the list commit the phase commit (change YA)" "$(yn roles_in_order)"
    roles_ctl() { local LIST1=$RUNNER1; roles_in_order; }
    check "control: roles_in_order refuses the runner commit named as the list commit" "$(ynnot roles_ctl)"
    roles_ctl2() { local RULES1=$DOC1; roles_in_order; }
    check "control: roles_in_order refuses the doc commit named as the rules commit" "$(ynnot roles_ctl2)"
    echo
    echo "phase: Milestone 4 part 1 P1 ($SPEC) negative.log; run at $(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ)"
    if [ -n "${RERUN_NOTE:-}" ]; then
      echo "rerun: $RERUN_NOTE"
      echo "rerun: the superseded files are kept in <superseded>"
      echo "command: sha256sum and wc -c over each superseded attempt named above"
      for sf in ${RERUN_FILES:-}; do
        if [ -f "$sf" ]; then
          printf '    %s  %s bytes  %s\n' "$(sha256sum "$sf" | cut -d' ' -f1)" "$(wc -c <"$sf")" "$(basename "$sf")"
        else
          printf '    NO HASH EXISTS for %s: the file is not there at run time\n' "$(basename "$sf")"
        fi
      done
      if [ -z "${RERUN_FILES:-}" ]; then echo "    RERUN_FILES was not set, so no attempt above is hashed by this run"; fi
    fi
    n_cargo=$(pgrep -x cargo | wc -l)
    n_rustc=$(pgrep -x rustc | wc -l)
    echo "SERIALIZED: no other log run was in flight; cargo processes at entry: $n_cargo; rustc processes at entry: $n_rustc; this log builds nothing: it runs the phase commit's scripts over fictional fixtures, with the gate's DEBUG binaries copied (changes YB and YC)"
    no_build_running() { [ "$((n_cargo + n_rustc))" = 0 ]; }
    check "no cargo or rustc process was running at entry" "$(yn no_build_running)"
    echo "root: <repo> stands for the repository's top level (inside <home>, so scrubbed first); scripts/counting.sh, scripts/excel-check-list.py and $SPEC are read from it by git show at the phase commit, the working spec is hashed in it, and it is cloned (read only) into <scratch>/w/clone; nothing in it is run or built"
    echo "root: <home> stands for the home directory of the account that ran this script"
    echo "root: <phase> stands for <home>/.cache/emend-build/m4p1-p1, the phase's directory on the disk; <phase>/logs/gate.log is the gate log read for the DEBUG binaries' hashes"
    echo "root: <scripts> stands for <phase>/scripts, this script's own directory on the disk, which holds the script and, in <scripts>/out, its own standard error (specs/0017 P1 verification ruling 7)"
    echo "root: <target> stands for <phase>/target, the phase's one target root (specs/0001); <target>/main/debug holds the gate's DEBUG binaries, read only (copied, never run in place)"
    echo "root: <build> stands for the build root on the disk, <phase>/negative unless NEGATIVE_BUILD names another"
    echo "root: <superseded> stands for <phase>/superseded, where a rerun's superseded files are kept; read only"
    echo "root: <scratch>/w stands for this run's mktemp -d working directory under <build>, kept on the disk after the run (change YC): <scratch>/w/counting.sh the phase commit's library, sourced; <scratch>/w/excel-check-list.py the phase commit's list script, run; <scratch>/w/clone a clone of <repo> checked out at the phase commit, whose scripts/passthrough-run.sh (with scripts/lo-check.sh beside it, which it sources) is run; <scratch>/w/bin the copied binaries; <scratch>/w/fx the fictional fixtures and the program that builds them; the cases' outputs beside them"
    echo "root: <close> stands for a directory of an earlier phase's log and appears in this log only in the printed script's carried history; this log reads and writes nothing under it (change YD)"
    echo "root: <gatebuild> stands for a directory of an earlier phase's log and appears in this log only in the printed script's carried history; this log reads and writes nothing under it (change YD)"
    apart() { case "$1/" in "$2"/*) return 1 ;; esac; case "$2/" in "$1"/*) return 1 ;; esac; return 0; }
    roots_distinct() { apart "$BUILD_ROOT" "$MAIN" && apart "$BUILD_ROOT" "$SELF_DIR" && apart "$MAIN" "$SELF_DIR" && apart "$SUPERSEDED_DIR" "$SELF_DIR" && apart "$SUPERSEDED_DIR" "$BUILD_ROOT" && apart "$SUPERSEDED_DIR" "$MAIN" && apart "$TARGET_DIR" "$BUILD_ROOT" && apart "$TARGET_DIR" "$MAIN"; }
    check "the roots other than <home> and <phase> are distinct, none inside another (<scratch>/w inside <build>)" "$(yn roots_distinct)"
    roots_ctl() { apart "$B" "$BUILD_ROOT"; }
    check "control: apart refuses <scratch>/w against <build>, which holds it" "$(ynnot roots_ctl)"
    echo "ANTHROPIC_API_KEY set in this shell: $([ -n "${ANTHROPIC_API_KEY:-}" ] && echo yes || echo no)"
    key_unset() { [ -z "${ANTHROPIC_API_KEY:-}" ]; }
    check "ANTHROPIC_API_KEY is not set" "$(yn key_unset)"
    key_set_ctl() { local ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=planted; key_unset; }
    check "control: the same check refuses a shell where the variable is set" "$(ynnot key_set_ctl)"
    echo "at entry: status lines $entry_all; of them this phase's logs or planning's spec $entry_ours; the lines:"
    git status --porcelain | sed 's/^/    /'
    echo "  the phase commit given to this script: $C"
    echo "  git rev-parse HEAD at this run:        $(git rev-parse HEAD)"
    echo "  the controls' commits: the null commit $NULL_SHA; the fabricated commit $FAKE_SHA (the given one, its last hex digit changed); the short sha $SHORT_SHA"
    check "the phase commit given to this script is a full sha" "$(yn full_sha "$C")"
    check "the phase commit given to this script is HEAD" "$(yn head_is "$C")"
    check "control: the same comparison refuses the null commit" "$(ynnot given_ok "$NULL_SHA")"
    check "control: the same comparison refuses a fabricated commit" "$(ynnot given_ok "$FAKE_SHA")"
    check "control: the same comparison refuses a short sha of the real commit" "$(ynnot given_ok "$SHORT_SHA")"
    short_names_head() { [ "$(git rev-parse "$SHORT_SHA")" = "$(git rev-parse HEAD)" ]; }
    check "control: the short sha is the real commit's, so only its form is refused" "$(yn short_names_head)"
    ours_ctl() { printf ' M scripts/check.sh\n' | LC_ALL=C "$G" -qE "$OURS"; }
    check "control: the phase's status pattern refuses a modified gate file" "$(ynnot ours_ctl)"
    ours_spec_ctl() { printf ' M specs/0029-engineering-close.md\n' | LC_ALL=C "$G" -qE "$OURS"; }
    check "control: the phase's status pattern refuses a modified spec other than specs/0030" "$(ynnot ours_spec_ctl)"
    ours_roadmap_ctl() { printf ' M ROADMAP.md\n' | LC_ALL=C "$G" -qE "$OURS"; }
    check "control: the phase's status pattern refuses a modified ROADMAP.md (committed and clean in this phase)" "$(ynnot ours_roadmap_ctl)"
    ours_rec_ctl() { printf '?? records/logs/m4p1-p1/gate.log\n?? records/logs/m3p7-p5/gate.log\n?? records/m4p1-p1-printarea-2026-01-01-%s.md\n?? records/logs/m4p1-p1x/gate.log\n M ROADMAP.md\n M specs/0030-carries-and-api-changes.md\n?? specs/0030-carries-and-api-changes.md\n' "$SHORT_SHA" | count_matches -E "$OURS" /dev/stdin; }
    check "control: the phase's status pattern takes this phase's log and planning's modified spec alone, no other phase's log, no record, no sibling directory and no untracked spec (change YA)" "$(yn [ "$(ours_rec_ctl)" = 2 ])"
    SPEC_HAVE=$(sha256sum <"$SPEC" | cut -d' ' -f1)
    SPEC_BLOB=$(git show "$C:$SPEC" | sha256sum | cut -d' ' -f1)
    echo "command: git show <phase commit>:$SPEC | sha256sum: $SPEC_BLOB; sha256sum $SPEC (the working file, printed and not asserted: planning holds its rulings during P1 uncommitted; change YA): $SPEC_HAVE"
    check "the spec's blob at the phase commit is at the hash this log pins" "$(yn [ "$SPEC_BLOB" = "$SPEC_WANT" ])"
    spec_ctl() { [ "$(printf 'x' | sha256sum | cut -d' ' -f1)" = "$SPEC_WANT" ]; }
    check "control: the same comparison refuses the hash of another file" "$(ynnot spec_ctl)"
    echo
    echo "## script that wrote this log"
    sed 's/^/    /' "$SELF"
    echo
    echo "## the shared counting library this log sources"
    sed 's/^/    /' "$B/counting.sh"
    echo
    echo "## counting controls"
    counting_controls
    if [ "$counting_controls_ok" != yes ]; then fail=1; fi
    
    echo
    echo "## the scripts under test, at the phase commit (change YB)"
    echo "command: git show <phase commit>:scripts/excel-check-list.py > <scratch>/w/excel-check-list.py; sha256sum: $(sha256sum <"$LIST" | cut -d' ' -f1)"
    sed 's/^/    /' "$LIST"
    echo "command: git clone -q <repo> <scratch>/w/clone; git -C <scratch>/w/clone checkout -q --detach <phase commit> (a scratch copy: nothing in <repo> is written)"
    git clone -q "$MAIN" "$B/clone" >"$B/clone.out" 2>"$B/clone.err" </dev/null
    rc_clone=$?
    git -C "$B/clone" checkout -q --detach "$C" >>"$B/clone.out" 2>>"$B/clone.err" </dev/null
    rc_co=$?
    CLONE_HEAD=$(git -C "$B/clone" rev-parse HEAD 2>/dev/null)
    echo "  clone exit $rc_clone; checkout exit $rc_co; the clone's HEAD: ${CLONE_HEAD:-none}; output bytes $(wc -c <"$B/clone.out"), standard error bytes $(wc -c <"$B/clone.err")"
    clone_ok() { [ "$rc_clone" = 0 ] && [ "$rc_co" = 0 ] && [ "$CLONE_HEAD" = "$C" ]; }
    check "the clone is at the phase commit" "$(yn clone_ok)"
    clone_ctl() { local CLONE_HEAD=$RUNNER1; clone_ok; }
    check "control: the same check refuses a clone at the runner commit" "$(ynnot clone_ctl)"
    clone_clean() { [ -z "$(git -C "$B/clone" status --porcelain 2>/dev/null)" ]; }
    check "the clone's working tree is clean" "$(yn clone_clean)"
    RUNNER=$B/clone/scripts/passthrough-run.sh
    runner_same() { [ "$(sha256sum <"$RUNNER" | cut -d' ' -f1)" = "$(git show "$C:scripts/passthrough-run.sh" | sha256sum | cut -d' ' -f1)" ]; }
    check "the clone's scripts/passthrough-run.sh is the phase commit's blob" "$(yn runner_same)"
    echo "command: sha256sum <scratch>/w/clone/scripts/passthrough-run.sh: $(sha256sum <"$RUNNER" | cut -d' ' -f1)"
    sed 's/^/    /' "$RUNNER"
    echo "command: sha256sum <scratch>/w/clone/scripts/lo-check.sh (sourced by the runner; no case gives --libreoffice, so its check never runs): $(sha256sum <"$B/clone/scripts/lo-check.sh" | cut -d' ' -f1)"
    
    echo
    echo "## the gate's DEBUG binaries, copied and checked against the gate log (change YB)"
    BIN=$B/bin
    mkdir -p "$BIN" || exit 2
    bins_ok=yes
    gate_there() { [ -f "$GATE_LOG" ]; }
    check "the gate log is there" "$(yn gate_there)"
    # gate_sha <name>: the hash gate.log printed for <target>/main/debug/<name>,
    # from its one line of the form "<target>/main/debug/<name>: sha256 <hash>,
    # bytes N"; empty unless there is exactly one.
    gate_sha() {
      local n
      n=$(count_matches -F "<target>/main/debug/$1: sha256 " "$GATE_LOG")
      [ "$n" = 1 ] || return 0
      LC_ALL=C "$G" -F "<target>/main/debug/$1: sha256 " "$GATE_LOG" | sed -e 's/^.*: sha256 //' -e 's/,.*$//'
    }
    for b in emend-copy xlsx-diff; do
      want=$(gate_sha "$b")
      cp "$TARGET_DIR/main/debug/$b" "$BIN/$b" 2>/dev/null || bins_ok=no
      have=$(sha256sum <"$BIN/$b" 2>/dev/null | cut -d' ' -f1)
      echo "command: grep -F '<target>/main/debug/$b: sha256 ' <phase>/logs/gate.log: ${want:-not exactly one line}; cp <target>/main/debug/$b <scratch>/w/bin/$b; sha256sum: ${have:-none}"
      bin_ok() { [ -n "$want" ] && [ "$have" = "$want" ] && [ -x "$BIN/$b" ]; }
      if bin_ok; then :; else bins_ok=no; fi
    done
    check "each copy is executable and hashes as gate.log printed for the binary it copies" "$(yn [ "$bins_ok" = yes ])"
    gate_sha_ctl() { [ -n "$(gate_sha no-such-binary)" ]; }
    check "control: the same reading finds no hash for a binary gate.log does not name" "$(ynnot gate_sha_ctl)"
    
    echo
    echo "## the fictional fixtures (change YC)"
    FX=$B/fx
    mkdir -p "$FX" || exit 2
    cat >"$FX/mkfix.py" <<'MKFIX'
    """Builds the negative log's fictional fixtures under the directory given:
    for the list script, two corpus roots (root1, root2), a keep and its
    open-check record; for the runner, a third corpus root (rroot) whose manifest
    has the runner's exact header and two workbooks with 12-hex ids. Nothing here
    is read from a corpus; every name and value is made up. An error row has no
    output file, as the runner leaves none for a refused input."""
    import hashlib
    import os
    import sys
    import zipfile
    
    top = sys.argv[1]
    CT = ('<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?>'
          '<Types xmlns="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/package/2006/content-types">'
          '<Default Extension="rels" ContentType="application/vnd.openxmlformats-package.relationships+xml"/>'
          '<Default Extension="xml" ContentType="application/xml"/>'
          '<Override PartName="/xl/workbook.xml" ContentType="application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.spreadsheetml.sheet.main+xml"/>'
          '<Override PartName="/xl/worksheets/sheet1.xml" ContentType="application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.spreadsheetml.worksheet+xml"/>'
          '</Types>')
    RELS = ('<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?>'
            '<Relationships xmlns="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/package/2006/relationships">'
            '<Relationship Id="rId1" Type="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/officeDocument" Target="xl/workbook.xml"/>'
            '</Relationships>')
    WB = ('<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?>'
          '<workbook xmlns="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/spreadsheetml/2006/main" '
          'xmlns:r="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships">'
          '<sheets><sheet name="Fixture" sheetId="1" r:id="rId1"/></sheets></workbook>')
    WB_RELS = ('<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?>'
               '<Relationships xmlns="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/package/2006/relationships">'
               '<Relationship Id="rId1" Type="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/worksheet" Target="worksheets/sheet1.xml"/>'
               '</Relationships>')
    
    
    def sheet(with_formula):
        cells = '<c r="A1"><v>3</v></c>'
        if with_formula:
            cells += '<c r="B1"><f>A1*2</f><v>6</v></c>'
        return ('<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?>'
                '<worksheet xmlns="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/spreadsheetml/2006/main">'
                '<sheetData><row r="1">' + cells + '</row></sheetData></worksheet>')
    
    
    def workbook(path, with_formula):
        with zipfile.ZipFile(path, "w", zipfile.ZIP_DEFLATED) as z:
            z.writestr("[Content_Types].xml", CT)
            z.writestr("_rels/.rels", RELS)
            z.writestr("xl/workbook.xml", WB)
            z.writestr("xl/_rels/workbook.xml.rels", WB_RELS)
            z.writestr("xl/worksheets/sheet1.xml", sheet(with_formula))
    
    
    def sha(path):
        return hashlib.sha256(open(path, "rb").read()).hexdigest()
    
    
    SUMMARY_HEAD = ("id", "label", "producer", "tags", "outcome", "exit", "signal", "wall_s", "peak_rss_kb",
                    "in_bytes", "out_bytes")
    MANIFEST_HEAD = ("id", "sha256", "bytes", "ext", "producer", "producer_version", "app", "source", "tags",
                     "added", "original_name", "note")
    
    
    def corpus_root(root, ids, runs):
        os.makedirs(os.path.join(root, "corpus"))
        rows = ["\t".join(MANIFEST_HEAD)]
        for wid, formula in ids:
            path = os.path.join(root, "corpus", wid + ".xlsx")
            workbook(path, formula)
            rows.append("\t".join((wid, sha(path), str(os.path.getsize(path)), "xlsx", "fixture", "-", "-",
                                   "fictional", "fictional", "2026-01-01", "-", "-")))
        with open(os.path.join(root, "manifest.tsv"), "w") as h:
            h.write("\n".join(rows) + "\n")
        for name, outcomes, summary in runs:
            run = os.path.join(root, "runs", name)
            lines = ["\t".join(SUMMARY_HEAD)]
            for (wid, formula), outcome in zip(ids, outcomes):
                os.makedirs(os.path.join(run, wid))
                src = os.path.join(root, "corpus", wid + ".xlsx")
                out_bytes = ""
                if outcome == "ok":
                    out = os.path.join(run, wid, "out.host.xlsx")
                    workbook(out, formula)
                    out_bytes = str(os.path.getsize(out))
                code = {"ok": "0", "error": "2", "timeout": "124"}[outcome]
                lines.append("\t".join((wid, "host", "fixture", "-", outcome, code, "-", "0.01", "1",
                                        str(os.path.getsize(src)), out_bytes)))
            if summary:
                with open(os.path.join(run, "summary.host.tsv"), "w") as h:
                    h.write("\n".join(lines) + "\n")
    
    
    LIST_IDS = (("fx-a", True), ("fx-b", False))
    corpus_root(os.path.join(top, "root1"), LIST_IDS,
                (("run-formula", ("ok", "ok"), True),
                 ("run-plain", ("ok", "ok"), True),
                 ("run-nosummary", ("ok", "ok"), False),
                 ("run-allerror", ("error", "error"), True),
                 ("run-timeout", ("ok", "timeout"), True)))
    corpus_root(os.path.join(top, "root2"), LIST_IDS, (("run-other", ("ok", "ok"), True),))
    corpus_root(os.path.join(top, "rroot"), (("fa0000000001", True), ("fa0000000002", False)), ())
    keep = os.path.join(top, "keep")
    os.makedirs(keep)
    rec = ["\t".join(("test", "in_bytes", "in_sha256", "out_bytes", "out_sha256", "lo_in", "lo", "reason_in",
                      "reason_out"))]
    for test, formula in (("t1", True), ("t2", False)):
        fin = os.path.join(keep, test + ".in.xlsx")
        fout = os.path.join(keep, test + ".out.xlsx")
        workbook(fin, formula)
        workbook(fout, not formula)
        rec.append("\t".join((test, str(os.path.getsize(fin)), sha(fin), str(os.path.getsize(fout)), sha(fout),
                              "ok", "ok", "none", "none")))
    with open(os.path.join(top, "open.txt"), "w") as h:
        h.write("\n".join(rec) + "\n")
    MKFIX
    echo "command: python3 -I <scratch>/w/fx/mkfix.py <scratch>/w/fx (the program, printed below, sha256 $(sha256sum <"$FX/mkfix.py" | cut -d' ' -f1))"
    sed 's/^/    /' "$FX/mkfix.py"
    python3 -I "$FX/mkfix.py" "$FX" >"$B/mkfix.out" 2>"$B/mkfix.err" </dev/null
    rc=$?
    echo "  exit: $rc; output bytes $(wc -c <"$B/mkfix.out"); standard error bytes $(wc -c <"$B/mkfix.err")"
    mkfix_ok() { [ "$rc" = 0 ] && [ ! -s "$B/mkfix.out" ] && [ ! -s "$B/mkfix.err" ]; }
    check "the fixtures were built: exit 0, nothing printed" "$(yn mkfix_ok)"
    echo "command: find <scratch>/w/fx -type f, the paths below it, sorted"
    (cd "$FX" && find . -type f | LC_ALL=C sort) | sed 's/^/    /'
    fx_there() {
      local f
      for f in root1/manifest.tsv root1/corpus/fx-a.xlsx root1/corpus/fx-b.xlsx \
        root1/runs/run-formula/summary.host.tsv root1/runs/run-plain/summary.host.tsv \
        root1/runs/run-allerror/summary.host.tsv root1/runs/run-timeout/summary.host.tsv \
        root1/runs/run-nosummary/fx-a/out.host.xlsx root2/manifest.tsv root2/runs/run-other/summary.host.tsv \
        rroot/manifest.tsv rroot/corpus/fa0000000001.xlsx rroot/corpus/fa0000000002.xlsx \
        keep/t1.in.xlsx keep/t2.out.xlsx open.txt; do
        [ -f "$FX/$f" ] || return 1
      done
    }
    check "every fixture file the cases read is there" "$(yn fx_there)"
    # outcome_count <summary> <outcome>: rows of that outcome, the column found by its header name.
    outcome_count() { awk -F'\t' -v w="$2" 'NR == 1 { for (i = 1; i <= NF; i++) if ($i == "outcome") o = i; next } $o == w { n++ } END { print n + 0 }' "$1"; }
    echo "  rows by outcome (the outcome column found by name): run-formula ok $(outcome_count "$FX/root1/runs/run-formula/summary.host.tsv" ok); run-plain ok $(outcome_count "$FX/root1/runs/run-plain/summary.host.tsv" ok); run-allerror error $(outcome_count "$FX/root1/runs/run-allerror/summary.host.tsv" error); run-timeout ok $(outcome_count "$FX/root1/runs/run-timeout/summary.host.tsv" ok) timeout $(outcome_count "$FX/root1/runs/run-timeout/summary.host.tsv" timeout)"
    fx_outcomes() { [ "$(outcome_count "$FX/root1/runs/run-formula/summary.host.tsv" ok)" = 2 ] && [ "$(outcome_count "$FX/root1/runs/run-plain/summary.host.tsv" ok)" = 2 ] && [ "$(outcome_count "$FX/root1/runs/run-allerror/summary.host.tsv" error)" = 2 ] && [ "$(outcome_count "$FX/root1/runs/run-timeout/summary.host.tsv" timeout)" = 1 ]; }
    check "the fixture summaries hold the outcomes the cases need" "$(yn fx_outcomes)"
    fx_outcomes_ctl() { [ "$(outcome_count "$FX/root1/runs/run-plain/summary.host.tsv" error)" = 1 ]; }
    check "control: the same count reads no error row in run-plain" "$(ynnot fx_outcomes_ctl)"
    MANIFEST_HEAD_WANT=$(git show "$C:scripts/passthrough-run.sh" | sed -n "s/^readonly MANIFEST_HEADER=\\$'\\(.*\\)'\$/\\1/p" | sed 's/\\t/|/g')
    MANIFEST_HEAD_HAVE=$(head -n 1 "$FX/rroot/manifest.tsv" | tr '\t' '|')
    echo "  the runner's manifest header (from its source, tabs as |): $MANIFEST_HEAD_WANT"
    echo "  the runner fixture's manifest header (tabs as |):          $MANIFEST_HEAD_HAVE"
    mh_ok() { [ -n "$MANIFEST_HEAD_WANT" ] && [ "$MANIFEST_HEAD_WANT" = "$MANIFEST_HEAD_HAVE" ]; }
    check "the runner fixture's manifest header is the one the runner checks" "$(yn mh_ok)"
    mh_ctl() { local MANIFEST_HEAD_HAVE=id; mh_ok; }
    check "control: the same comparison refuses another header" "$(ynnot mh_ctl)"
    
    echo
    echo "## the messages and the note's form, read from the sources at the phase commit (never typed)"
    echo "command: grep -o -E 'Refused\\(\"<the message's start>[^\"]*\"\\)' <scratch>/w/excel-check-list.py; grep -o -E '\"note: %d [^\"]*\"' the same (the format string the script prints, not its docstring's description); grep -o -E 'die \"the run directory holds an earlier run[^\"]*\"' <scratch>/w/clone/scripts/passthrough-run.sh; the text between the quotes"
    msg_of() { LC_ALL=C "$G" -o -E "Refused\\(\"$1[^\"]*\"\\)" "$LIST" | sed -e 's/^Refused("//' -e 's/")$//'; }
    MSG_NOOK=$(msg_of 'a run directory holds no ')
    MSG_OUTCOME=$(msg_of 'a workbook of the run has an outcome ')
    MSG_NOSUMMARY=$(msg_of 'the run directory has no ')
    MSG_OTHERROOT=$(msg_of 'a --run-plain directory is under ')
    NOTE_FMT=$(LC_ALL=C "$G" -o -E '"note: %d [^"]*"' "$LIST" | sed -e 's/^"//' -e 's/"$//')
    MSG_EARLIER=$(LC_ALL=C "$G" -o -E 'die "the run directory holds an earlier run[^"]*"' "$RUNNER" | sed -e 's/^die "//' -e 's/"$//')
    echo "  the list's no-ok-row message: $MSG_NOOK"
    echo "  the list's outcome message: $MSG_OUTCOME"
    echo "  the list's no-summary message: $MSG_NOSUMMARY"
    echo "  the list's other-root message: $MSG_OTHERROOT"
    echo "  the list's note form: $NOTE_FMT"
    echo "  the runner's earlier-run message: $MSG_EARLIER"
    one_line_msg() { [ -n "$1" ] && [ "$(printf '%s\n' "$1" | wc -l)" = 1 ]; }
    msgs_ok() { one_line_msg "$MSG_NOOK" && one_line_msg "$MSG_OUTCOME" && one_line_msg "$MSG_NOSUMMARY" && one_line_msg "$MSG_OTHERROOT" && one_line_msg "$NOTE_FMT" && one_line_msg "$MSG_EARLIER"; }
    check "each message and the note's form is found once in its source and is not empty" "$(yn msgs_ok)"
    msg_ctl() { one_line_msg "$(msg_of 'no such message ')"; }
    check "control: the same reading finds no message for a start the source does not hold" "$(ynnot msg_ctl)"
    # note_line <n> <label>: the note the list script prints, built from its own form.
    note_line() { printf "$NOTE_FMT" "$1" "$2"; }
    echo "  the note for 1 row of run-x, built from the form: $(note_line 1 run-x)"
    
    # list_case <name> <--run> <--run-plain or -> [<root>]: the list script run in
    # the fixture root's directory with the keep and the record, into
    # <scratch>/w/out-<name>.txt; exit, stdout and stderr kept as <name>.rc/.out/.err.
    list_case() {
      local name=$1 run=$2 plain=$3 out=$B/out-$1.txt rc
      echo "### $name"
      check "$name: the list file is not there before the case" "$(ynnot test -e "$out")"
      if [ "$plain" = - ]; then
        echo "command: (cd <scratch>/w/fx && python3 -I <scratch>/w/excel-check-list.py --run $run --keep keep --record open.txt --out <scratch>/w/out-$name.txt)"
        (cd "$FX" && python3 -I "$LIST" --run "$run" --keep keep --record open.txt --out "$out") >"$B/$name.out" 2>"$B/$name.err" </dev/null
      else
        echo "command: (cd <scratch>/w/fx && python3 -I <scratch>/w/excel-check-list.py --run $run --run-plain $plain --keep keep --record open.txt --out <scratch>/w/out-$name.txt)"
        (cd "$FX" && python3 -I "$LIST" --run "$run" --run-plain "$plain" --keep keep --record open.txt --out "$out") >"$B/$name.out" 2>"$B/$name.err" </dev/null
      fi
      rc=$?
      echo "$rc" >"$B/$name.rc"
      echo "  exit: $rc; standard output:"
      sed 's/^/    /' "$B/$name.out"
      echo "  standard error:"
      sed 's/^/    /' "$B/$name.err"
    }
    rc_of() { cat "$B/$1.rc"; }
    # list_by <name> <column> <value>: rows of the case's list whose column holds the value.
    list_by() { awk -F'\t' -v c="$2" -v v="$3" 'NR > 1 && $c == v { n++ } END { print n + 0 }' "$B/out-$1.txt"; }
    # list_pair <name> <id> <label>: rows of the case's list with that id and that label.
    list_pair() { awk -F'\t' -v i="$2" -v l="$3" 'NR > 1 && $2 == i && $3 == l { n++ } END { print n + 0 }' "$B/out-$1.txt"; }
    # refused_as <name> <message>: exit 2, the one error line, nothing on stdout, no list.
    refused_as() { [ "$(rc_of "$1")" = 2 ] && [ "$(count_lines "$B/$1.err")" = 1 ] && [ "$(count_matches -xF "error: $2" "$B/$1.err")" = 1 ] && [ ! -s "$B/$1.out" ] && [ ! -e "$B/out-$1.txt" ]; }
    # listed_with <name> <stderr lines>: exit 0, one counts line on stdout, the list with mode 600, stderr of that many lines.
    listed_with() { [ "$(rc_of "$1")" = 0 ] && [ "$(count_lines "$B/$1.out")" = 1 ] && [ "$(count_matches -E '^corpus rows [0-9]+ ' "$B/$1.out")" = 1 ] && [ "$(stat -c %a "$B/out-$1.txt" 2>/dev/null)" = 600 ] && [ "$(count_lines "$B/$1.err")" = "$2" ]; }
    # plant_error <summary> <id>: the row of that id gets outcome error and an empty out_bytes, and its output goes, as the runner leaves it.
    plant_error() {
      awk -F'\t' -v OFS='\t' -v id="$2" 'NR == 1 { for (i = 1; i <= NF; i++) { if ($i == "outcome") o = i; if ($i == "out_bytes") b = i } print; next } $1 == id { $o = "error"; $b = "" } { print }' "$1" >"$1.planted" && mv "$1.planted" "$1" && rm "$(dirname "$1")/$2/out.host.xlsx"
    }
    
    # runner_case <name> <out dir> <ids file or -> [<label>]: the clone's runner over
    # <scratch>/w/fx/rroot with the copied binaries, no --libreoffice, TMPDIR in
    # <scratch>/w/tmp; exit, stdout and stderr kept as <name>.rc/.out/.err.
    mkdir -p "$B/tmp" || exit 2
    runner_case() {
      local name=$1 out=$2 ids=$3 label=${4:-host} rc
      echo "### $name"
      if [ "$ids" = - ]; then
        echo "command: TMPDIR=<scratch>/w/tmp bash <scratch>/w/clone/scripts/passthrough-run.sh --label $label --passthrough <scratch>/w/bin/emend-copy --xlsx-diff <scratch>/w/bin/xlsx-diff --corpus <scratch>/w/fx/rroot --out ${out/#$FX/<scratch>/w/fx}"
        TMPDIR=$B/tmp bash "$RUNNER" --label "$label" --passthrough "$BIN/emend-copy" --xlsx-diff "$BIN/xlsx-diff" --corpus "$FX/rroot" --out "$out" >"$B/$name.out" 2>"$B/$name.err" </dev/null
      else
        echo "command: TMPDIR=<scratch>/w/tmp bash <scratch>/w/clone/scripts/passthrough-run.sh --label $label --passthrough <scratch>/w/bin/emend-copy --xlsx-diff <scratch>/w/bin/xlsx-diff --corpus <scratch>/w/fx/rroot --out ${out/#$FX/<scratch>/w/fx} --ids ${ids/#$B/<scratch>/w}"
        TMPDIR=$B/tmp bash "$RUNNER" --label "$label" --passthrough "$BIN/emend-copy" --xlsx-diff "$BIN/xlsx-diff" --corpus "$FX/rroot" --out "$out" --ids "$ids" >"$B/$name.out" 2>"$B/$name.err" </dev/null
      fi
      rc=$?
      echo "$rc" >"$B/$name.rc"
      echo "  exit: $rc; standard output:"
      sed 's/^/    /' "$B/$name.out"
      echo "  standard error:"
      sed 's/^/    /' "$B/$name.err"
    }
    printf 'fa0000000001\n' >"$B/ids-one"
    echo "command: printf 'fa0000000001\\n' > <scratch>/w/ids-one (the --ids file selecting the first runner fixture alone)"
    # runner_refused <name> <out dir>: exit 2, the one error line from the source, nothing on stdout,
    # the summary holding its header alone and no output in any subdirectory.
    runner_refused() { [ "$(rc_of "$1")" = 2 ] && [ "$(count_lines "$B/$1.err")" = 1 ] && [ "$(count_matches -xF "error: $MSG_EARLIER" "$B/$1.err")" = 1 ] && [ ! -s "$B/$1.out" ] && [ "$(count_lines "$2/summary.host.tsv")" = 1 ] && [ -z "$(find "$2" -name 'out.host.*' -print -quit)" ]; }
    # runner_ran <name> <out dir> <rows>: exit 0, nothing on stderr, the summary with that many rows, every one ok.
    runner_ran() { [ "$(rc_of "$1")" = 0 ] && [ ! -s "$B/$1.err" ] && [ "$(count_lines "$2/summary.host.tsv")" = "$(($3 + 1))" ] && [ "$(outcome_count "$2/summary.host.tsv" ok)" = "$3" ]; }
    
    echo
    echo "## N1 to N3: the list script's refusals and its error-row count"
    list_case N1 root1/runs/run-allerror -
    check "N1: a --run directory of error rows only: refused with the no-ok-row message, nothing written" "$(yn refused_as N1 "$MSG_NOOK")"
    list_case N1b root1/runs/run-formula root1/runs/run-allerror
    check "N1b: the same directory as --run-plain: refused the same way" "$(yn refused_as N1b "$MSG_NOOK")"
    echo "command: cp -a <scratch>/w/fx/root1/runs/run-plain <scratch>/w/fx/root1/runs/run-plain-n2; the fx-a row planted as error (out_bytes emptied, its out.host.xlsx removed)"
    cp -a "$FX/root1/runs/run-plain" "$FX/root1/runs/run-plain-n2" || exit 2
    plant_error "$FX/root1/runs/run-plain-n2/summary.host.tsv" fx-a || exit 2
    echo "  run-plain-n2: error rows $(outcome_count "$FX/root1/runs/run-plain-n2/summary.host.tsv" error); ok rows $(outcome_count "$FX/root1/runs/run-plain-n2/summary.host.tsv" ok)"
    list_case N2 root1/runs/run-formula root1/runs/run-plain-n2
    NOTE_N2=$(note_line 1 run-plain-n2)
    echo "  the note expected on stderr: $NOTE_N2"
    echo "  N2: fx-a under run-plain-n2 $(list_pair N2 fx-a run-plain-n2); fx-b under run-plain-n2 $(list_pair N2 fx-b run-plain-n2)"
    # n2_ok <case> <label>: exit 0, the note for 1 row of the label alone on stderr, fx-a left out and fx-b kept under it.
    n2_ok() { listed_with "$1" 1 && [ "$(count_matches -xF "$(note_line 1 "$2")" "$B/$1.err")" = 1 ] && [ "$(list_pair "$1" fx-a "$2")" = 0 ] && [ "$(list_pair "$1" fx-b "$2")" = 1 ]; }
    check "N2: the planted error row is left out and counted: exit 0, the note alone on stderr" "$(yn n2_ok N2 run-plain-n2)"
    list_case N2c root1/runs/run-formula root1/runs/run-plain
    echo "  N2c: fx-a under run-plain $(list_pair N2c fx-a run-plain); fx-b under run-plain $(list_pair N2c fx-b run-plain)"
    n2c_ok() { listed_with N2c 0 && [ "$(list_pair N2c fx-a run-plain)" = 1 ] && [ "$(list_pair N2c fx-b run-plain)" = 1 ]; }
    check "N2c (the control): the unplanted directory: exit 0, no note, both rows listed" "$(yn n2c_ok)"
    check "control: the N2 check refuses N2c's output" "$(ynnot n2_ok N2c run-plain)"
    list_case N3 root1/runs/run-formula root1/runs/run-timeout
    check "N3: a timeout row (neither ok nor error) is still refused with the outcome message" "$(yn refused_as N3 "$MSG_OUTCOME")"
    list_case L1 root1/runs/run-nosummary -
    check "L1: a --run directory with no summary.host.tsv: refused with the no-summary message" "$(yn refused_as L1 "$MSG_NOSUMMARY")"
    list_case L2 root1/runs/run-formula root2/runs/run-other
    check "L2: a --run-plain directory under another corpus root: refused with the other-root message" "$(yn refused_as L2 "$MSG_OTHERROOT")"
    check "control: the refusal check refuses N2c's output (a passing case)" "$(ynnot refused_as N2c "$MSG_NOOK")"
    check "control: the refusal check refuses N1's output read against another message" "$(ynnot refused_as N1 "$MSG_OUTCOME")"
    
    echo
    echo "## N4: the runner refuses a run directory holding an earlier, stopped run (decision 1 A, ruling 2)"
    R=$FX/rroot/runs
    SUMMARY_HEAD=$(sed -n "s/^readonly SUMMARY_HEADER=\\$'\\(.*\\)'\$/\\1/p" "$RUNNER")
    echo "command: sed -n <readonly SUMMARY_HEADER=...> <scratch>/w/clone/scripts/passthrough-run.sh, the header the runner writes (its escapes as in the source): $SUMMARY_HEAD"
    check "the summary header is read from the runner's source" "$(yn [ -n "$SUMMARY_HEAD" ])"
    mkdir -p "$R/n4/fa0000000001/lo.host" "$R/n4c" || exit 2
    printf '%b\n' "$SUMMARY_HEAD" >"$R/n4/summary.host.tsv" || exit 2
    printf '%b\n' "$SUMMARY_HEAD" >"$R/n4c/summary.host.tsv" || exit 2
    echo "command: mkdir -p <scratch>/w/fx/rroot/runs/n4/fa0000000001/lo.host <scratch>/w/fx/rroot/runs/n4c; printf '%b\\n' <the header> into each directory's summary.host.tsv (an earlier run stopped before its first row)"
    N4_BEFORE=$(sha256sum <"$R/n4/summary.host.tsv" | cut -d' ' -f1)
    echo "  n4/summary.host.tsv before: sha256 $N4_BEFORE, lines $(count_lines "$R/n4/summary.host.tsv")"
    runner_case N4 "$R/n4" "$B/ids-one"
    N4_AFTER=$(sha256sum <"$R/n4/summary.host.tsv" | cut -d' ' -f1)
    echo "  n4/summary.host.tsv after: sha256 $N4_AFTER"
    check "N4: refused: exit 2, the earlier-run message alone, no row, no output" "$(yn runner_refused N4 "$R/n4")"
    check "N4: the summary is byte for byte as before" "$(yn [ "$N4_AFTER" = "$N4_BEFORE" ])"
    check "N4: the planted directory is still there (the runner removed nothing)" "$(yn test -d "$R/n4/fa0000000001/lo.host")"
    runner_case N4c "$R/n4c" "$B/ids-one"
    check "N4c (the control): the same directory without the plant runs: exit 0, one ok row" "$(yn runner_ran N4c "$R/n4c" 1)"
    check "control: the refusal check refuses N4c's output" "$(ynnot runner_refused N4c "$R/n4c")"
    
    echo
    echo "## N5: the rewritten removals stop on an unset variable; the old forms widen (ruling 3)"
    N5=$B/n5
    mkdir -p "$N5/cwd" || exit 2
    : >"$N5/cwd/sentinel" || exit 2
    # The patterns are built from pieces, so no unguarded removal is spelled
    # whole in this script's own text.
    RM=rm
    RMF="$RM -rf"
    NEW_DIR_PAT="$RMF \"\${dir:?}/lo."
    NEW_TRAP_PAT="trap '$RMF \"\${WORK:?}\"' EXIT"
    OLD_DIR_PAT="$RMF \"\$dir/lo."
    OLD_TRAP_PAT="trap '$RMF \"\$WORK\"' EXIT"
    git show "$RUNNER1^:scripts/passthrough-run.sh" >"$N5/runner-old.sh" || exit 2
    echo "command: git show <the runner commit>^:scripts/passthrough-run.sh > <scratch>/w/n5/runner-old.sh (the runner before this phase); sha256 $(sha256sum <"$N5/runner-old.sh" | cut -d' ' -f1)"
    NEW_DIR_N=$(count_matches -F "$NEW_DIR_PAT" "$RUNNER")
    NEW_TRAP_N=$(count_matches -xF "$NEW_TRAP_PAT" "$RUNNER")
    OLD_DIR_N=$(count_matches -F "$OLD_DIR_PAT" "$N5/runner-old.sh")
    OLD_TRAP_N=$(count_matches -xF "$OLD_TRAP_PAT" "$N5/runner-old.sh")
    OLD_DIR_IN_NEW=$(count_matches -F "$OLD_DIR_PAT" "$RUNNER")
    OLD_TRAP_IN_NEW=$(count_matches -xF "$OLD_TRAP_PAT" "$RUNNER")
    echo "  lines of each form: the new loop removal in the phase commit's runner $NEW_DIR_N; the new trap $NEW_TRAP_N; the old loop removal in the old runner $OLD_DIR_N; the old trap $OLD_TRAP_N; the old forms in the phase commit's runner $OLD_DIR_IN_NEW and $OLD_TRAP_IN_NEW"
    forms_ok() { [ "$NEW_DIR_N" = 1 ] && [ "$NEW_TRAP_N" = 1 ] && [ "$OLD_DIR_N" = 1 ] && [ "$OLD_TRAP_N" = 1 ] && [ "$OLD_DIR_IN_NEW" = 0 ] && [ "$OLD_TRAP_IN_NEW" = 0 ]; }
    check "each form is one line where it should be, and the old forms are gone from the phase commit's runner" "$(yn forms_ok)"
    NEW_DIR_CMD=$(LC_ALL=C "$G" -F "$NEW_DIR_PAT" "$RUNNER" | sed -e 's/^ *//' -e 's/ || die .*$//')
    NEW_TRAP_CMD=$(LC_ALL=C "$G" -xF "$NEW_TRAP_PAT" "$RUNNER" | sed -e "s/^trap '//" -e "s/' EXIT\$//")
    OLD_DIR_CMD=$(LC_ALL=C "$G" -F "$OLD_DIR_PAT" "$N5/runner-old.sh" | sed -e 's/^ *//' -e 's/ || die .*$//')
    OLD_TRAP_CMD=$(LC_ALL=C "$G" -xF "$OLD_TRAP_PAT" "$N5/runner-old.sh" | sed -e "s/^trap '//" -e "s/' EXIT\$//")
    echo "  the new loop removal, as run: $NEW_DIR_CMD"
    echo "  the new trap's command, as run: $NEW_TRAP_CMD"
    # The old forms are never printed (the scan refuses an unruled removal in any
    # log line): each is named by its line number in the old runner and the
    # sha256 of the line, and shown below only as its printf plant.
    line_no() { LC_ALL=C "$G" -n "$1" "$2" "$3" | cut -d: -f1; }
    echo "  the old loop removal: line $(line_no -F "$OLD_DIR_PAT" "$N5/runner-old.sh") of <scratch>/w/n5/runner-old.sh, the line's sha256 $(LC_ALL=C "$G" -F "$OLD_DIR_PAT" "$N5/runner-old.sh" | sha256sum | cut -d' ' -f1)"
    echo "  the old trap: line $(line_no -xF "$OLD_TRAP_PAT" "$N5/runner-old.sh") of the same, the line's sha256 $(LC_ALL=C "$G" -xF "$OLD_TRAP_PAT" "$N5/runner-old.sh" | sha256sum | cut -d' ' -f1)"
    # stop_case <name> <variable> <command>: the command in a child bash from
    # <scratch>/w/n5/cwd with the variable unset and LABEL=host.
    stop_case() {
      local name=$1 var=$2 cmd=$3 rc
      printf '%s\n' "$cmd" >"$N5/$name.sh"
      echo "command: (cd <scratch>/w/n5/cwd && env -u $var LABEL=host bash <scratch>/w/n5/$name.sh); the file holds: $cmd"
      (cd "$N5/cwd" && env -u "$var" LABEL=host bash "$N5/$name.sh") >"$B/$name.out" 2>"$B/$name.err" </dev/null
      rc=$?
      echo "$rc" >"$B/$name.rc"
      echo "  exit: $rc; standard output bytes $(wc -c <"$B/$name.out"); standard error:"
      sed 's/^/    /' "$B/$name.err"
    }
    # stopped <name> <variable>: non-zero exit, nothing printed, bash's message naming the variable, the sentinel kept.
    stopped() { [ "$(rc_of "$1")" != 0 ] && [ ! -s "$B/$1.out" ] && [ "$(count_lines "$B/$1.err")" = 1 ] && [ "$(count_matches -F "$2: parameter null or not set" "$B/$1.err")" = 1 ] && [ -f "$N5/cwd/sentinel" ]; }
    stop_case N5a dir "$NEW_DIR_CMD"
    check "N5a: the loop removal with dir unset stops before rm runs: non-zero exit, the message naming dir, the sentinel kept" "$(yn stopped N5a dir)"
    stop_case N5b WORK "$NEW_TRAP_CMD"
    check "N5b: the trap's removal with WORK unset stops the same way" "$(yn stopped N5b WORK)"
    # The old forms, planted on a scratch copy with the removal replaced by printf.
    PRINTF_FORM="printf '%s\\n'"
    OLD_DIR_PLANT=${OLD_DIR_CMD/#$RMF/$PRINTF_FORM}
    OLD_TRAP_PLANT=${OLD_TRAP_CMD/#$RMF/$PRINTF_FORM}
    plant_safe() { case "$1" in "$PRINTF_FORM "*) return 0 ;; esac; return 1; }
    check "the old loop form's plant starts with printf, not a removal" "$(yn plant_safe "$OLD_DIR_PLANT")"
    check "the old trap form's plant starts with printf, not a removal" "$(yn plant_safe "$OLD_TRAP_PLANT")"
    check "control: the same check refuses the unplanted old form" "$(ynnot plant_safe "$OLD_DIR_CMD")"
    if plant_safe "$OLD_DIR_PLANT"; then stop_case N5c dir "$OLD_DIR_PLANT"; fi
    if plant_safe "$OLD_TRAP_PLANT"; then stop_case N5d WORK "$OLD_TRAP_PLANT"; fi
    echo "  N5c printed (the old loop form's arguments with dir unset):"
    sed 's/^/    /' "$B/N5c.out"
    widened() { [ "$(rc_of N5c)" = 0 ] && [ "$(head -n 1 "$B/N5c.out")" = /lo.host ] && [ "$(sed -n 2p "$B/N5c.out")" = /lo-in.host ] && [ ! -s "$B/N5c.err" ]; }
    check "N5c: the old loop form does not stop: with dir unset its arguments widen to /lo.host and /lo-in.host" "$(yn widened)"
    empty_arg() { [ "$(rc_of N5d)" = 0 ] && [ "$(wc -c <"$B/N5d.out")" = 1 ] && [ ! -s "$B/N5d.err" ]; }
    check "N5d: the old trap form does not stop: with WORK unset its argument is empty" "$(yn empty_arg)"
    check "control: the stop check refuses N5c's output" "$(ynnot stopped N5c dir)"
    check "control: the stop check refuses N5a read against another variable" "$(ynnot stopped N5a WORK)"
    
    echo
    echo "## the log itself"
    ALL=()
    for f in "$B"/*.out "$B"/*.err; do ALL+=("$f"); done
    nbytes=$(cat "${ALL[@]}" | LC_ALL=C tr -cd '\000-\010\013\014\016-\037\177\200-\377' | wc -c)
    printf 'x \033[31mred\033[m y caf\303\251\n' >"$B/ctl-bytes"
    nctl=$(LC_ALL=C tr -cd '\000-\010\013\014\016-\037\177\200-\377' <"$B/ctl-bytes" | wc -c)
    echo "  captured outputs: ${#ALL[@]}; control and non-ASCII bytes in them: $nbytes; in a control file with two ESC and one two-byte character: $nctl"
    check "no control or non-ASCII byte in any captured output" "$(yn [ "$nbytes" = 0 ])"
    check "control: the same count finds four bytes in the control file" "$(yn [ "$nctl" = 4 ])"
    echo "command: git status --porcelain"
    git status --porcelain | sed 's/^/    /'
    st_all=$(run_lines git status --porcelain)
    st_ours=$(run_matches -E "$OURS" git status --porcelain)
    echo "status lines: $st_all; of them this phase's logs or planning's spec: $st_ours"
    status_accounted() { [ "$st_all" != error ] && [ "$st_ours" != error ] && [ "$st_all" = "$st_ours" ]; }
    check "every change in the working tree is this phase's logs or planning's uncommitted spec" "$(yn status_accounted)"
    echo
    echo "## this script's own standard error (specs/0017 P1 verification ruling 7)"
    nerr=$(wc -c <"$ERR")
    cp "$ERR" "$B/c-err"; printf 'x' >>"$B/c-err"
    nerr_ctl=$(wc -c <"$B/c-err")
    echo "  command: wc -c <scripts>/out/negative.stderr: $nerr bytes; a copy with one byte appended: $nerr_ctl"
    check "this script wrote nothing to its standard error" "$(yn [ "$nerr" = 0 ])"
    check "control: the same count reads one byte more in the copy" "$(yn [ "$nerr_ctl" = "$((nerr + 1))" ])"
    echo "run ended at $(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ)"
    echo "negative log checks all as expected: $([ "$fail" -eq 0 ] && echo yes || echo no)"
    exec 1>&3
    wait "$SCRUB_PID" 2>/dev/null
    exit "$fail"

## the shared counting library this log sources
    # emend shared counting forms (specs/0007-gate-self-test.md, "scripts/counting.sh").
    #
    # Sourced by scripts/gate-self-test.sh and by the log scripts of later phases,
    # which use these instead of carrying their own copy.
    #
    # WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. In T3 P4 four counting sites compared a bare `grep -c`
    # with 0 without reading the exit status, so a read error printed 0 and the
    # check passed. Two independent checks found them, and the class was declared
    # closed three further times while instances still stood: a helper whose first
    # repair read grep's status while `cat` was the failing command, and both
    # halves of one function in T2 P7. Planning's remedy is that every counting
    # form, including a throwaway one, is a named function that reads the exit
    # status and has a positive control, from the start.
    #
    # Every function prints either a number or the word "error". None of them ever
    # prints a matched line, a value, or a path. A caller that compares a result
    # with 0 therefore cannot mistake a failed read for an absence.
    #
    # counting_controls runs every form against a match, a miss and an unreadable
    # file, prints one line per control, and sets counting_controls_ok. Callers run
    # it before using any form.
    
    G=/usr/bin/grep
    
    # count_matches <-F|-E> <pattern> <file>...: total matching lines across the
    # files, or "error" if any file could not be read. The files go to grep
    # directly: with `cat file | grep -c`, an unreadable file makes CAT fail while
    # grep counts 0 over the empty stream and exits 1, so the read error reads as
    # "no hits".
    count_matches() {
      local mode=$1 pattern=$2 out rc
      shift 2
      out=$(LC_ALL=C "$G" -a -H -c "$mode" -e "$pattern" -- "$@" 2>/dev/null); rc=$?
      if [ "$rc" -ge 2 ]; then printf 'error'; return; fi
      printf '%s\n' "$out" | awk -F: '{ n += $NF } END { print n + 0 }'
    }
    
    # count_from_file <-F|-E> <pattern file> <file>...: the same, with the patterns
    # read from a file (grep -f), so a pattern is never printed.
    count_from_file() {
      local mode=$1 patterns=$2 out rc
      shift 2
      out=$(LC_ALL=C "$G" -a -H -c "$mode" -f "$patterns" -- "$@" 2>/dev/null); rc=$?
      if [ "$rc" -ge 2 ]; then printf 'error'; return; fi
      printf '%s\n' "$out" | awk -F: '{ n += $NF } END { print n + 0 }'
    }
    
    # count_bytes <tr class> <file>...: total bytes of that class across the files,
    # or "error" if any file could not be read. Each file is read on its own rather
    # than through `cat "$@" | tr`, for the same reason as above.
    count_bytes() {
      local class=$1 total=0 f n
      shift
      for f in "$@"; do
        [ -r "$f" ] || { printf 'error'; return; }
        n=$(LC_ALL=C tr -cd "$class" <"$f" | wc -c) || { printf 'error'; return; }
        total=$((total + n))
      done
      printf '%s' "$total"
    }
    
    # count_lines <file>...: total lines, or "error" if any file could not be read.
    count_lines() {
      local total=0 f n
      for f in "$@"; do
        [ -r "$f" ] || { printf 'error'; return; }
        n=$(wc -l <"$f") || { printf 'error'; return; }
        total=$((total + n))
      done
      printf '%s' "$total"
    }
    
    # count_matches_i <-F|-E> <pattern> <file>...: count_matches with -i, the form
    # the hygiene step uses for OFFICE_RE and ZONE_RE. It lived in a log script as a
    # hand-rolled copy with no control of its own (independent check 2, T6 P1, gap
    # 14); a counting form belongs here, with a control, even a throwaway one.
    count_matches_i() {
      local mode=$1 pattern=$2 out rc
      shift 2
      out=$(LC_ALL=C "$G" -a -H -c -i "$mode" -e "$pattern" -- "$@" 2>/dev/null); rc=$?
      if [ "$rc" -ge 2 ]; then printf 'error'; return; fi
      printf '%s\n' "$out" | awk -F: '{ n += $NF } END { print n + 0 }'
    }
    
    # run_lines <command...>: run the command, and print the number of lines it
    # wrote on stdout, or "error" if the command itself failed. The log scripts
    # asserted a clean working tree with
    #   git status --porcelain >file 2>/dev/null || : >file
    # which turns a FAILED git status into an empty file, so the check read a
    # failed command as a clean tree (independent check 2, T6 P1, gap 1; planning
    # reproduced it). Reading the command's exit status is the whole point: a
    # command that could not run is never the same as a command that found nothing.
    run_lines() {
      local out rc
      out=$("$@" 2>/dev/null); rc=$?
      if [ "$rc" -ne 0 ]; then printf 'error'; return; fi
      if [ -z "$out" ]; then printf '0'; return; fi
      printf '%s\n' "$out" | wc -l | tr -d ' '
    }
    
    # run_matches <-F|-E> <pattern> <command...>: the same, counting only the lines
    # that match, so a caller can compare "all lines" with "lines of the expected
    # shape" without either count hiding a failed command.
    run_matches() {
      local mode=$1 pattern=$2 out rc
      shift 2
      out=$("$@" 2>/dev/null); rc=$?
      if [ "$rc" -ne 0 ]; then printf 'error'; return; fi
      if [ -z "$out" ]; then printf '0'; return; fi
      printf '%s\n' "$out" | LC_ALL=C "$G" -a -c "$mode" -e "$pattern" || true
    }
    
    CTRL_CLASS='\000-\010\013\014\016-\037\177'
    NONASCII_CLASS='\200-\377'
    
    # counting_controls <label prefix>: run every counting form above against a
    # fixture that must match and one that must not, and against an unreadable
    # file, printing one line per result. Sets counting_controls_ok to yes or no.
    # This is the positive control planning requires for every form, and it also
    # proves each form reports "error" rather than 0 when it cannot read.
    counting_controls() {
      local d ok=yes r
      d=$(mktemp -d) || { counting_controls_ok=no; return; }
      printf 'alpha\nbeta needle gamma\nneedle\n' >"$d/hit"
      printf 'nothing of interest\n' >"$d/miss"
      printf 'x \033[31mred\033[m\017 y\ncaf\303\251\n' >"$d/bytes"
      printf 'needle\n' >"$d/patterns"
      printf 'unreadable\n' >"$d/locked"
      chmod 000 "$d/locked"
    
      r=$(count_matches -F needle "$d/hit");        echo "  control count_matches on a file with two matching lines: $r (expect 2)"; [ "$r" = 2 ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_matches -F needle "$d/miss");       echo "  control count_matches on a file with none: $r (expect 0)"; [ "$r" = 0 ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_matches -F needle "$d/hit" "$d/miss"); echo "  control count_matches summed over two files: $r (expect 2)"; [ "$r" = 2 ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_matches -E 'ne+dle' "$d/hit");      echo "  control count_matches with a regular expression: $r (expect 2)"; [ "$r" = 2 ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_matches -F needle "$d/locked");     echo "  control count_matches on an unreadable file: $r (expect error)"; [ "$r" = error ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_matches -F needle "$d/hit" "$d/locked"); echo "  control count_matches on readable plus unreadable: $r (expect error)"; [ "$r" = error ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_from_file -F "$d/patterns" "$d/hit"); echo "  control count_from_file on the same file: $r (expect 2)"; [ "$r" = 2 ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_from_file -F "$d/patterns" "$d/miss"); echo "  control count_from_file on a file with none: $r (expect 0)"; [ "$r" = 0 ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_from_file -F "$d/patterns" "$d/locked"); echo "  control count_from_file on an unreadable file: $r (expect error)"; [ "$r" = error ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_bytes "$CTRL_CLASS" "$d/bytes");    echo "  control count_bytes, control bytes in a file with ESC, ESC and SI: $r (expect 3)"; [ "$r" = 3 ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_bytes "$NONASCII_CLASS" "$d/bytes"); echo "  control count_bytes, non-ASCII bytes in a file with one two-byte character: $r (expect 2)"; [ "$r" = 2 ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_bytes "$CTRL_CLASS" "$d/miss");     echo "  control count_bytes on a clean file: $r (expect 0)"; [ "$r" = 0 ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_bytes "$CTRL_CLASS" "$d/locked");   echo "  control count_bytes on an unreadable file: $r (expect error)"; [ "$r" = error ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_lines "$d/hit");                    echo "  control count_lines on a three-line file: $r (expect 3)"; [ "$r" = 3 ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_lines "$d/locked");                 echo "  control count_lines on an unreadable file: $r (expect error)"; [ "$r" = error ] || ok=no
      printf 'NEEDLE\nneedle\n' >"$d/mixedcase"
      r=$(count_matches_i -F needle "$d/mixedcase"); echo "  control count_matches_i on mixed case: $r (expect 2)"; [ "$r" = 2 ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_matches -F needle "$d/mixedcase");  echo "  control count_matches on the same file, case sensitive: $r (expect 1)"; [ "$r" = 1 ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_matches_i -F needle "$d/locked");   echo "  control count_matches_i on an unreadable file: $r (expect error)"; [ "$r" = error ] || ok=no
      r=$(run_lines printf 'a\nb\nc\n');            echo "  control run_lines on a command printing three lines: $r (expect 3)"; [ "$r" = 3 ] || ok=no
      r=$(run_lines true);                          echo "  control run_lines on a command printing nothing: $r (expect 0)"; [ "$r" = 0 ] || ok=no
      r=$(run_lines false);                         echo "  control run_lines on a FAILING command: $r (expect error)"; [ "$r" = error ] || ok=no
      r=$(run_lines "$d/no-such-command");          echo "  control run_lines on a command that cannot run: $r (expect error)"; [ "$r" = error ] || ok=no
      r=$(run_matches -F needle printf 'needle\nother\n'); echo "  control run_matches on a command printing one match: $r (expect 1)"; [ "$r" = 1 ] || ok=no
      r=$(run_matches -F needle false);             echo "  control run_matches on a FAILING command: $r (expect error)"; [ "$r" = error ] || ok=no
    
      chmod 644 "$d/locked"
      rm -rf "${d:?}"
      counting_controls_ok=$ok
      echo "  counting controls: $([ "$ok" = yes ] && echo "all as expected" || echo FAILED)"
    }

